SteamStub ownership is handled inside SLSsteam's existing
GetAppOwnershipTicketExtendedData hook. When Steam has no genuine ticket for a
managed added app, SLSsteam derives the response from its cached AppID 7
ownership ticket and inserts the requested AppID immediately before the
signature. Genuine successful Steam responses and unmanaged apps are never
modified. This avoids patching game executables and requires no bundled
Steamless runtime. The bounded ticket transformer has a native regression test.
The complete path was also accepted live with AppID 250180 (Metal Slug 3): the
installed executable remained byte-identical, no backup or patch marker was
created, and the game reached playable state under Proton.

Manifest history resolution uses the optional browser helper under
`assets/steamdb-history-extension/`. The helper exports only information
rendered by SteamDB in an explicitly opened browser session: build transitions,
all rendered depot sections and their categories/ownership/descriptions/Size/DL
values, branches, DLC relationships, raw depot change events, and targeted
per-depot manifest histories for shared/redistributable payloads.
`slssteam-control` treats that file as an untrusted observation: it anchors the
newest build to Steam's current local depot map, verifies every transition
while walking backwards, and installs the cache atomically only if the entire
observed chain joins. DLC and shared releases inside a base-build interval
attach to its preceding build. Shared/redistributable depots are deliberately
not required in the Lua-derived anchor: stplug-in files supply decryption keys,
not a complete Steam depot topology. A shared history must instead be declared
by the root app and cover every root-build interval. It does not claim coverage
before SteamDB's first available build or across pages that failed to load.

The hosted Game Builds page exposes only whole-app build selection. It resolves
the selected build to its complete internal depot map, persists the lock, then
uses the declared `tsuki.steam.app.validate` host import to ask Steam to apply
the change. Selecting Live removes the lock and validates back to Steam's
public build. SteamDB history JSON remains an explicit page-level import.

Configuration files are forward-compatible. Missing optional collection
sections mean an empty collection. Missing scalar settings use their declared
safe defaults. `slssteam-control` is the sole document writer: its revisioned
`settings.get`/`settings.set` exports preserve unknown fields and replace the
document atomically, while Manifest Pins use the same writer. The Ronin
settings document covers every active scalar consumed by SLSsteam, including
MaxSchemaTries, DisableUpdates, and DumpClientInterfaces. Malformed YAML and
genuine parsing failures remain user-visible.

Steam's native Date Added value for a discovered application comes from
`cache/ronin-first-seen.yaml`. The package prelaunch phase records an AppID only
on its first discovery and retains that history across removal, restoration,
and source-file edits. Explicit `SubscriptionTimestamps` values override the
automatic record. The injected module consumes the cache read-only during its
initial loader-time configuration pass.

The Steam compatibility hash-list refresh runs curl outside Steam's TLS
process. It inherits the user's proxy, DNS, certificate, and HOME environment
but removes LD_AUDIT, LD_PRELOAD, and LD_LIBRARY_PATH before exec. Logs name
the attempted source and clearly distinguish a successful refresh, an empty
response, a curl failure, cache fallback, and parse failure.
